Species and description
The shrubs reach {{convert|0.5|-|6|m|ftin|abbr=on}} tall, rarely up to {{convert|10|m|ft}} in central Asia. The leaf arrangement can be alternate or opposite. 'Plants of the World Online includes the following species:
Hippophae × goniocarpa {{Au|Y.S.Lian & al. ex Swenson & Bartish}} is a natural hybrid of H. neurocarpa and H. sinensis which grows in mountainous regions in Nepal, Mongolia and China. 
Hippophae gyantsensis {{Au|(Rousi) Y.S.Lian}}
Hippophae litangensis {{Au|Y.S.Lian & Xue L.Chen ex Swenson & Bartish}}
Hippophae neurocarpa {{Au|S.W.Liu & T.N.He}}
Hippophae rhamnoides {{Au|L.}}: Common sea buckthorn has dense and stiff branches, and is very thorny. The leaves are a distinct pale silvery-green, lanceolate, {{convert|3|-|8|cm|in|frac=2|abbr=on}} long, and less than {{convert|7|mm|in|frac=16|abbr=on}} broad. It is dioecious, with separate male and female plants. The male produces brownish flowers which produce wind-distributed pollen. The female plants produce orange berries {{convert|6|-|9|mm|in|frac=16|abbr=on}} in diameter, soft, juicy, and rich in oils. The roots distribute rapidly and extensively, providing a nonleguminous nitrogen fixation role in surrounding soils.
Hippophae salicifolia {{Au|D.Don}} (willow-leaved sea buckthorn) is restricted to the Himalayas, to the south of the common sea buckthorn, growing at high altitudes in dry valleys; it differs from H. rhamnoides in having broader (to {{convert|10|mm|in|frac=8|abbr=on}}){{citation needed|date=June 2023}} and greener (less silvery) leaves, and yellow berries. A wild variant occurs in the same area, but at even higher altitudes in the alpine zone.{{Citation needed|date=April 2007}} It is a low shrub not growing taller than {{convert|1|m|ftin|abbr=on}} with small leaves {{convert|1|-|3|cm|in|frac=4|abbr=on}} long.{{citation needed|date=July 2017}}
Hippophae sinensis {{Au|(Rousi) Tzvelev}}
Hippophae tibetana {{Au|Schltdl.}}
